<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-invertible-matrix-theorem" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Invertible Matrix Theorem</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We already discussed
      <xref ref="thm-matrix-inverses-equivalences" text="type-global" />, and in what
      follows we will be building on it. The result below gathers together, in a single
      list, every characterization of invertibility we have met so far: in terms of row
      reduction, pivots, linear independence, spanning, solvability of
      <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, one-sided inverses, and the transpose.
    </p>
    <p>
      Throughout this section, <m>T_A</m> denotes the linear transformation
      <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> associated with the matrix <m>A</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-imt-statement">
    <title>Statement of the Theorem</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem">
      <title>The Invertible Matrix Theorem</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix, and let <m>\vec{X}</m>,
          <m>\vec{B}</m> be <m>n \times 1</m> vectors. The following conditions are
          equivalent.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p><m>A</m> is invertible.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>A\vec{X} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution,
                <m>\vec{X} = \vec{0}</m>.
                (Equivalently, <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is one-to-one.)
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>A</m> can be transformed to <m>I_n</m> by elementary row operations.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p><m>A</m> has <m>n</m> pivot positions.</p></li>
            <li><p>The columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The columns of <m>A</m> span <m>\R^n</m>.
                (Equivalently, <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is onto.)
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The system <m>A\vec{X} = \vec{B}</m> has a unique solution <m>\vec{X}</m>
                for any choice of <m>\vec{B}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                There exists an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>C</m> with the property that
                <m>AC = I_n</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                There exists an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>D</m> with the property that
                <m>DA = I_n</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p><m>A^T</m> is invertible.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <remark xml:id="rem-imt-one-sided">
      <p>
        Conditions (8) and (9) are worth pausing over. They say that for
        <em>square</em> matrices, a one-sided inverse is automatically a two-sided
        inverse. This is what completes the argument left unfinished in the proof of
        <xref ref="thm-matrix-inverses-equivalences" text="type-global" />.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-imt-proof">
    <title>Proof of the Equivalences</title>

    <p>
      <term>(1) <m>\Longleftrightarrow</m> (2).</term>
      We already discussed that if (1) holds, then the reduced row-echelon matrix of
      <m>A</m> will be <m>I_n</m>, which means <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> implies
      <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>, which is (2). Conversely, if (2) holds, then the system
      has no free variables, so after row reduction there is a leading one in every row.
      Since the matrix <m>A</m> is <m>n \times n</m>, its reduced row-echelon matrix is
      therefore <m>I_n</m>, and hence <m>A</m> is invertible.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>(1) <m>\Longleftrightarrow</m> (3).</term>
      We already discussed this equivalence.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>(1) <m>\Longleftrightarrow</m> (4).</term>
      We have also already discussed this equivalence. Note that the reduced row-echelon
      matrix being <m>I_n</m> is the same as having <m>n</m> pivot positions.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>(2) <m>\Longleftrightarrow</m> (5).</term>
      We proved a few sessions ago that the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent
      if and only if <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> implies <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>(3) <m>\Longleftrightarrow</m> (6).</term>
      If <m>A</m> can be transformed to <m>I_n</m>, then <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is a
      consistent system of linear equations for all <m>\vec{b} \in \R^n</m>, and hence the
      columns of <m>A</m> span <m>\R^n</m>; i.e., for all <m>\vec{b}</m> in <m>\R^n</m> we
      can find a linear combination
      <md>
        x_1 \vec{A}_1 + x_2 \vec{A}_2 + \cdots + x_n \vec{A}_n = \vec{b}.
      </md>
      Conversely, if the columns of <m>A</m> span <m>\R^n</m>, then
      <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is a consistent system for all
      <m>\vec{b} \in \R^n</m>, and the last row of the row-echelon matrix cannot be all
      zeros. This means there are <m>n</m> leading ones, and hence the reduced
      row-echelon matrix is <m>I_n</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <term>(3) <m>\Longleftrightarrow</m> (7).</term>
      If <m>A</m> can be reduced to <m>I_n</m>, then we know that the solution to
      <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> exists uniquely for all <m>\vec{b} \in \R^n</m>.
      Conversely, if <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> has a unique solution for any
      <m>\vec{b} \in \R^n</m>, then the reduced row-echelon matrix cannot have a row of
      zeros, which means <m>I_n</m> is the reduced row-echelon matrix.
    </p>


    <p>
      <term>(1) <m>\Longleftrightarrow</m> (10).</term>
      If <m>A</m> is invertible, then
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \left( A A^{-1} \right)^T \amp = I^T
          \amp \amp \Longrightarrow \amp
          \left( A^{-1} \right)^T A^T \amp = I,
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \left( A^{-1} A \right)^T \amp = I^T
          \amp \amp \Longrightarrow \amp
          A^T \left( A^{-1} \right)^T \amp = I,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      so <m>A^T</m> is invertible and
      <m>\left( A^T \right)^{-1} = \left( A^{-1} \right)^T</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Conversely, if <m>A^T</m> is invertible, then transposing each of
      <m>\left( A^T \right)^{-1} A^T = I</m> and
      <m>A^T \left( A^T \right)^{-1} = I</m> gives
      <md>
        <mrow>
          A \left( \left( A^T \right)^{-1} \right)^T \amp = I,
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \left( \left( A^T \right)^{-1} \right)^T A \amp = I,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      so <m>\left( \left( A^T \right)^{-1} \right)^T = A^{-1}</m> and <m>A</m> is
      invertible.
    </p>
    <p xml:id="intro-imt-parsons">
      <em>Build the two proofs.</em> In each Parsons exercise, arrange the valid
      blocks into a proof, leaving one incorrect block unused. Begin with the
      forward implication from (1), then prove the converse using the earlier
      equivalences. Throughout, <m>A</m>, <m>C</m>, and <m>D</m> are
      <m>n\times n</m> matrices and vectors are columns in <m>\R^n</m>.
      Compare the constructions: a right inverse supplies a preimage, while a
      left inverse lets us recover equal inputs from equal outputs.
      Drag blocks to the proof column, or focus a block, press Space to pick it
      up, use the arrow keys to move it, and press Space to drop it. Use
      <em>Check</em> for feedback and <em>Reset</em> to start again.
    </p>
    <paragraphs xml:id="imt-parsons-proofs">
      <title>Reconstruct the One-Sided Inverse Proofs</title>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-imt-1-8-parsons" language="natural" indentation="show">
        <title><m>(1)\Longleftrightarrow(8)</m>: A Right Inverse</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if
            <m>AC=I_n</m> for some <m>C</m>. For the converse, show that
            <m>T_A</m> is onto.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <blocks randomize="yes">
          <block><p>Assume <m>A</m> is invertible. Take <m>C=A^{-1}</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>Then <m>AC=AA^{-1}=I_n</m>. This proves <m>(1)\Rightarrow(8)</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>Conversely, suppose <m>AC=I_n</m> for some <m>C</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>Let <m>\vec{b}\in\R^n</m> be arbitrary and set <m>\vec{x}=C\vec{b}</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>By associativity, <m>A\vec{x}=(AC)\vec{b}=\vec{b}</m>.</p></block>
          <block>
            <p>
              Thus <m>T_A</m> is onto. By <m>(6)\Rightarrow(1)</m>, <m>A</m>
              is invertible, proving <m>(8)\Rightarrow(1)</m>.
            </p>
          </block>
          <block correct="no">
            <p>Instead set <m>\vec{x}=\vec{b}C</m>; multiplying in either order gives the same column vector.</p>
          </block>
        </blocks>
        <hint>
          <p>
            To prove onto, start with an arbitrary output <m>\vec{b}</m> and
            construct an input using <m>C</m>. Check the matrix dimensions:
            <m>C\vec{b}</m> is a column vector. Do not assume that
            <m>A^{-1}</m> exists in the converse.
          </p>
        </hint>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-imt-1-9-parsons" language="natural" indentation="show">
        <title><m>(1)\Longleftrightarrow(9)</m>: A Left Inverse</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Prove that <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if
            <m>DA=I_n</m> for some <m>D</m>. For the converse, show that
            <m>T_A</m> is one-to-one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <blocks randomize="yes">
          <block><p>Assume <m>A</m> is invertible. Take <m>D=A^{-1}</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>Then <m>DA=A^{-1}A=I_n</m>. This proves <m>(1)\Rightarrow(9)</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>Conversely, suppose <m>DA=I_n</m> for some <m>D</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>Take <m>\vec{x}_1,\vec{x}_2\in\R^n</m> with <m>A\vec{x}_1=A\vec{x}_2</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>Multiply on the left by <m>D</m>: <m>DA\vec{x}_1=DA\vec{x}_2</m>.</p></block>
          <block><p>Since <m>DA=I_n</m>, this gives <m>\vec{x}_1=\vec{x}_2</m>.</p></block>
          <block>
            <p>
              Thus <m>T_A</m> is one-to-one. By <m>(2)\Rightarrow(1)</m>,
              <m>A</m> is invertible, proving <m>(9)\Rightarrow(1)</m>.
            </p>
          </block>
          <block correct="no">
            <p>Cancel <m>A</m> directly, since every square matrix can be canceled.</p>
          </block>
        </blocks>
        <hint>
          <p>
            In the converse, justify cancellation by multiplying on the
            <em>left</em> by <m>D</m> and using <m>DA=I_n</m>. Being square
            alone does not justify canceling <m>A</m>.
          </p>
        </hint>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-imt-applications">
    <title>Using the Theorem</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-imt-interactive">
      <caption>
        The theorem, geometrically, for the invertible matrix
        <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 3 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
        Drag <m>\vec{b}</m> anywhere: the system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> always has a
        solution (the columns span <m>\R^2</m>, olive green), and the solution set (deep wine) is
        always a single point (the columns are independent). Every statement of the
        theorem is a different way of saying what you see here.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-imt-interactive" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,2:1,3&amp;x=1,1&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-not-invertible-by-columns">
      <title>Detecting Non-Invertibility from the Columns</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the following matrix invertible?
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 5 \amp -1 \\
              1 \amp 0 \amp 7 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 2 \amp 10 \amp -2
            \end{bmatrix}
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Label the columns <m>\vec{u}_1, \vec{u}_2, \vec{u}_3, \vec{u}_4</m>. Since
          <md>
            \vec{u}_1 - \vec{u}_2
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
              - \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \vec{u}_4,
          </md>
          we know that the columns are not linearly independent, and hence the matrix is
          not invertible by condition (5) of
          <xref ref="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-true-false">
      <title>True or False?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>Invertible transformations map the zero vector to itself.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Non-invertible matrices cannot be written as a product of elementary
                matrices.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a) True.</term>
          Condition (2) of <xref ref="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem" text="type-global" />
          says that for invertible matrices we have <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> implies
          <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>. This means <m>T_A(\vec{0}) = \vec{0}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b) True.</term>
          If we assume that a non-invertible matrix <m>A</m> can be written as a product
          of elementary matrices, <m>A = E_1 E_2 \cdots E_n</m>, we can easily see that
          the product is invertible since each elementary matrix is invertible:
          <md>
            \left( E_1 E_2 \cdots E_n \right)^{-1}
            = E_n^{-1} E_{n-1}^{-1} \cdots E_2^{-1} E_1^{-1},
          </md>
          which means we reach a contradiction. Hence non-invertible matrices cannot be
          written as a product of elementary matrices.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-inverse-from-polynomial">
      <title>An Inverse from a Polynomial Identity</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>P</m> is a square matrix which satisfies the following:
          <md>
            P^3 + 2P^2 + 2P - 3I = 0.
          </md>
          Find <m>P^{-1}</m>, if possible.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Divide the identity by <m>3</m> and isolate <m>I</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \tfrac{1}{3}\left( P^3 + 2P^2 + 2P - 3I \right) \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \tfrac{1}{3}\left( P^3 + 2P^2 + 2P \right) - I \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \tfrac{1}{3}\left( P^3 + 2P^2 + 2P \right) \amp = I.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Factoring <m>P</m> out on the left gives
          <md>
            P \left[ \tfrac{1}{3} \left( P^2 + 2P + 2I \right) \right] = I,
          </md>
          so by condition (8) of
          <xref ref="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem" text="type-global" />, <m>P</m> is
          invertible and
          <md>
            P^{-1} = \tfrac{1}{3} \left( P^2 + 2P + 2I \right).
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
